  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Slender Club (Macrotyphula juncea) is a delicate, thread-like fungus characterized by its thin, wiry fruiting bodies that stand upright from decaying wood and leaf litter. This small saprotroph plays an important role in decomposing organic matter in forest ecosystems throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Thread-like structure",
      "description": "Extremely thin, wire-like fruiting bodies that are typically 2-4 inches tall",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Color",
      "description": "Dark brown to blackish fruiting bodies that may appear almost hair-like in appearance",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Growth pattern",
      "description": "Grows in clusters or groups from decaying wood chips, mulch, and leaf litter",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found on heavily decayed hardwood, wood chips, and rich leaf litter in moist forest floors and gardens throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its delicate, hair-like appearance, Slender Club is a powerful decomposer that helps break down tough woody materials that other fungi cannot easily access.",
